<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03WF">Warning <span data-tag="03WF">8.4.3.3</span>.</a> The conclusion of Theorem <a data-tag="04BE" href="/tag/04BE">8.4.3.2</a> is not necessarily satisfied if we assume only that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $\kappa $-small. For example, suppose that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= S$ is a set of cardinality $\kappa $ (regarded as a discrete simplicial set), and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be (the nerve of) the partially ordered set $\{  0 &lt; 1 \} $. Then we can identify objects of $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } )$ with collections of $\kappa $-small Kan complexes $\{  X_ s \} _{s \in S}$. Define a functor $\lambda : \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ by the formula </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \lambda ( \{  X_ s \} _{s \in S} ) = \begin{cases}  0 &amp;  \text{ if } | \{  s \in S: X_{s} \neq \emptyset \}  | &lt; \kappa \\  1 &amp;  \text{ otherwise, } \end{cases} \]
</div>
<p> and let $\lambda _0: \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be the constant functor taking the value $0$. The functors $\lambda $ and $\lambda _{0}$ both preserve $\kappa $-small colimits and coincide on the image of the Yoneda embedding $h_{\bullet }$, but do not coincide in general. </p>
</article>
